🔍 Analysis Phases
Phase 1 — Problem Definition

Mapped 7-table ERD to understand relationships
Defined 5 business questions to drive the analysis

Phase 2 — Data Exploration (EDA)

Loaded all CSVs in pandas, checked shape, dtypes, nulls, duplicates
Converted date columns from object to datetime64
Normalized customer names with .str.title() and emails with .str.lower()

Phase 3 — SQL Analysis

Loaded data into SQLite using sqlite3 + pandas
Wrote queries using JOINs, GROUP BY, CASE WHEN, HAVING, subqueries
Key queries: revenue by customer type, delivery rate, avg charges by service type

Phase 4 — Python Deep Analysis

Merged tables using pd.merge() with appropriate join types
Created derived columns: PaymentCategory, ShipmentEfficiency, CustomerAge, Delivery_days
Segment analysis: customer types, payment modes, shipment domains

Phase 5 — Excel Dashboard (4 sheets)
Sheet  | Contents 
Overall Dashboard | 8 KPI cards + 4 summary charts + 2 slicers
Customer Analysis | Revenue by type, payment mode split, top 10 customers, PaymentCategory distribution
Shipment & Logistics | Delivery status, domain breakdown, efficiency analysis, weight vs charges
Employee Performance | Top employees by shipments, branch headcount, designation breakdown

💡 Key Insights

1.Retail customers are the most valuable segment — generating ₹37L (39% of revenue) despite being slightly more in count. Wholesale customers contribute the least (₹23.6L) and may need targeted engagement.
2.50% delivery failure rate is a major operational red flag. Half the shipments are either not delivered or delayed — this directly correlates with the 50% unpaid payment rate.
3.COD is the dominant payment mode (55% of revenue) but carries higher non-payment risk. Encouraging card payments could reduce outstanding collections.
4.Domestic shipments outnumber international (109 vs 91) but international shipments tend to have higher charges — a potential growth segment worth investing in.
5.Shipment efficiency varies significantly across content categories — Healthcare and Luggage show the highest weight-to-charge ratios, while Electronics and Automotive are the least efficient.
